  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "title 'H2O' at top",
    "vertical axis labeled 'Pressure, p (atm)' with ticks at 0.006, 1, 218 (nonlinear/log-like spacing)",
    "horizontal axis labeled 'Temperature, T (°C)' with ticks at 0, 0.01, 100, 374 (nonlinear spacing)",
    "red fusion (melting) curve rising nearly vertically from the triple point, leaning slightly left with increasing pressure",
    "red vaporization (boiling) curve sweeping up and right from the triple point through (100 °C, 1 atm) to the critical point",
    "red sublimation curve continuing from the triple point toward the lower left",
    "filled dot labeled 'Triple point' at 0.006 atm, 0.01 °C with dashed guide lines to both axes",
    "filled dot labeled 'Critical point' at 218 atm, 374 °C with dashed guide lines to both axes",
    "dashed guide lines marking the crossing at 100 °C and 1 atm",
    "italic region labels: 's' (left, solid), 'l' (wedge between branches, liquid), 'v' (lower right, vapor)",
    "curved arrows labeling the branches 'Melting', 'Boiling', 'Sublimation'",
    "movable state-point marker showing current (T, p)",
    "highlight shading on the region containing the state point",
    "heating-path polylines: 1 atm path, low-pressure sublimation path, supercritical path"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "tempSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Temperature T (°C)",
      "range": [-50, 400, 1],
      "default": -20,
      "teaches": "Phase is read off the diagram by locating the point: moving T slides the state point horizontally and it changes phase only when it crosses a red coexistence curve"
    },
    {
      "id": "pressSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pressure p (atm)",
      "range": [0.001, 230, 0.001],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "Raising pressure at fixed temperature near 0 °C crosses the left-leaning melting line from solid into liquid — added pressure melts ice because the fusion curve has negative slope"
    },
    {
      "id": "heatingPath",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Heating path",
      "options": ["1 atm", "Low pressure", "Supercritical"],
      "default": "1 atm",
      "teaches": "The transition observed while heating depends on the path through p-T space: at 1 atm ice melts then boils at 100 °C, below the triple point it sublimes directly, and above 218 atm liquid and vapor merge continuously at the critical point with no boiling"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the map",
      "narration": "The red curves divide the diagram into solid (s), liquid (l), and vapor (v) regions. Drag the T and p sliders and watch the state point move — the phase label changes only when a red coexistence curve is crossed.",
      "state": { "tempSlider": -20, "pressSlider": 0.5, "heatingPath": "1 atm" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pressure melts ice",
      "narration": "Hold T near 0 °C and raise the pressure: the point crosses the left-leaning melting line and the phase flips from solid to liquid. Because this line slopes left, squeezing ice can melt it — unusual behavior shared by few substances.",
      "state": { "tempSlider": 0, "pressSlider": 50, "heatingPath": "1 atm" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three ways to heat",
      "narration": "Switch heating paths. At 1 atm the path crosses melting at 0 °C and boiling at 100 °C; the low-pressure path passes under the triple point so ice sublimes straight to vapor; the supercritical path goes around the critical point so liquid becomes vapor with no boiling at all.",
      "state": { "tempSlider": 100, "pressSlider": 1, "heatingPath": "Supercritical" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The coexistence curves are a map: any (T, p) point's phase is whichever region contains it, and the melting line's negative slope is what lets pressure melt ice while the critical point is where liquid–vapor distinction disappears.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Faithfully reproduce the OpenStax layout: bold 'H2O' title; both axes with visibly nonlinear tick spacing (0.006, 1, 218 on p; 0, 0.01, 100, 374 on T). Three red curves meeting at the triple-point dot; the fusion line nearly vertical but clearly leaning left toward higher pressure; the vaporization curve gently concave-up ending at the critical-point dot. Thin dashed gray guide lines from triple point, critical point, and the (100 °C, 1 atm) crossing to the axes. Italic serif 's', 'l', 'v' region labels; small curved arrows with 'Melting', 'Boiling', 'Sublimation' tags pointing at their branches. The state-point marker should be a distinct filled circle (e.g., dark blue) with a live coordinate readout and phase name; the occupied region should tint subtly (light blue/yellow/green per phase) as it moves. Heating paths drawn as dashed arrows at their respective pressures, labeled with the transitions crossed ('melting', 'boiling', 'sublimation', or 'no sharp transition')."
